  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1243 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nCumulus is beginning to develop along a couple of lines, one\nfrom around Fulton, KY towards the Wabash River, and the latter\nfrom Doniphan, MO towards Salem, IL closer to a frontal\nboundary. Dew points have reached the mid to upper 50s in SEMO\ninto far SW KY, and approach 55 across much of Southern\nIllinois, while the Evansville Tri-State and areas east of the\neastern line of cumulus have 48-52 dew points.\n\nOn the synoptic scale, a broad upper level trough is digging\ninto the Tennessee River Valley today. Surface low pressure is\ntracking east-southeastward through the Midwestern I-states\npassing to the north of the Quad State. Southwesterlies have \nbeen slower to ramp up than initially anticipated, but should \ngust to around 18-23mph this afternoon. These southwesterlies \nwill bring in some moisture, but from Central Texas rather than \nthe richer moisture from the Gulf that should stay south of \nhere. Nevertheless, dew points should rise to the mid-upper 50s \nacross most of the Quad State, with some 60 values in the south.\n\nThe new blend of short-range models with the REFS and HRRR \nproduce around 1000J/kg of MLCAPE mid-afternoon through sunset. \nEffective bulk shear rises to 35-50kts late afternoon, mainly in\nthe form of speed shear, while 0-3km shear is closer to 25kts. \nMid-level lapse rates hold at around 7C/km through the \nafternoon. Severe weather potential remains limited and \nuncertain. There is concern about how far north upper 50s-near \n60 dew points can reach, but the greater instability will be in \nthe north. There is also potential for post-sunset activity to \ntrend lighter fairly quickly, though that is more of a concern \neast of the Quad State. The main threat window locally for the \nWabash River/LBL area eastward is 3-730pm. Some models, \nespecially the HRRR, generate a few storms with the leading \ncloud band currently in the middle of the Quad State, but is \nvery unlikely to amount to much before exiting the region. Some \nof the REFS members and the HRRR develop a broken line of storms\nwith the trailing line of cumulus closer to the cold front. \nPoPs have been increased as there aren't really any completely \ndry runs anymore, though some models/ensemble members only have\nlimited sub-severe storm development. Damaging winds remain the\nprimary severe weather hazard, should a severe threat develop, \nthough a cellular storm producing hail is also possible. STP is \naround 1, with SRH around 150-200m2/s2, just enough to keep \ntornadoes from being ruled out entirely.\n\nA steady stream of northwesterly winds originating from the\nHudson Bay tonight through Saturday will bring much colder \ntemperatures. A weak upper shortwave passes through the trough\ntomorrow, which has resulted in more models producing a few\nshowers over the Quad State. PoPs have been expanded through all\nbut the southwestern corner, but amounts will be trivial and\nthunder is not anticipated. Highs top out in the lower half of\nthe 50s tomorrow before falling to around freezing tomorrow\nnight. Several days of cold weather are forecast with highs in \nthe upper 40s to lower 50s Friday to Monday and lows bottom out \nwell below freezing over the weekend. A cutoff low moving across\nthe Southern Plains over the weekend might clip the \nsouthwestern corner of the Quad State with a few light showers \nSunday night. Afterwards, dry conditions and ridging start next \nweek with a warming trend.",
